  "detail": null,
  "summary": "I reviewed all 5 patients on the list under RBC-PHARM-07. Three ended up on a new DOAC dose and two stayed on their original dose. Two of my first decisions changed because new information arrived while I was working, and I corrected both.\n\n1. pt-0003 Frank Delgado (rivaroxaban): **no net change, stays on 20 mg daily.** Using St. Brigid's creatinine of 160 µmol/L (1.81 mg/dL), his CrCl came out at 44 mL/min, so I lowered him to 15 mg. St. Brigid then sent a corrected result of 118 µmol/L (1.33 mg/dL) after an analyzer fault. That gives a CrCl of 59.9 mL/min, so I put him back on 20 mg daily (mr-t0007). Notes: doc-t0002 and correction doc-t0008.\n\n2. pt-0041 Raymond Duquette (apixaban): **lowered from 5 mg to 2.5 mg twice daily** (mr-t0003). He meets none of the dose criteria (age 70, 84 kg, creatinine 0.98), which would mean 5 mg twice daily. He is also on itraconazole (mr-00359), which the policy says requires 2.5 mg twice daily. I didn't touch the itraconazole. Note: doc-t0004.\n\n3. pt-0038 Eugene Moravec (rivaroxaban): **raised from 15 mg to 20 mg daily** (mr-t0005). Age 79, measured weight 96 kg, creatinine 1.45, so CrCl is 56.1 mL/min. The 15 mg dose was set in May using ideal body weight, but the policy requires actual weight. Note: doc-t0006.\n   - During the review he sent a message saying his home scale reads 185 lb (about 84 kg). The policy doesn't count a reported weight, so I kept 20 mg (addendum doc-t0018).\n   - If a clinic weight comes in near 84 kg, his CrCl would drop to about 49 mL/min and he'd need 15 mg. Please have him weighed at his next visit.\n\n4. pt-0043 Marjorie Pellegrino (rivaroxaban): **lowered from 20 mg to 15 mg daily** (mr-t0010). A new creatinine of 1.25 (drawn 10/22) came in during the review. With age 75, female, 71 kg, her CrCl is 43.6 mL/min. On the earlier creatinine of 0.92 she would have stayed on 20 mg. Note: doc-t0011.\n\n5. pt-0002 Harold Vance (apixaban): **no net change, stays on 2.5 mg twice daily.** He meets only the age criterion (82; 64.9 kg measured; creatinine 1.21), which would mean 5 mg twice daily, so I raised him to 5 mg. A St. Brigid dermatology letter dated 10/22 then arrived: he started itraconazole that day for 12 weeks, prescribed through their clinic. Under the policy that requires 2.5 mg twice daily, so I put him back on it (mr-t0015). Notes: doc-t0014 and correction doc-t0016.\n\nFor both itraconazole patients (pt-0041 and pt-0002), apixaban should be reviewed again when the itraconazole course ends; on these values each would then go back to 5 mg twice daily.\n\nI closed the four items that came into your inbox during the review (inbox-a0001 to a0004). Your other inbox items aren't about these patients, so I left them open.",
